[{:name "Version",
  :unstable false,
  :declarations
  [{:kind :define,
    :name "MINO_VERSION_MAJOR",
    :value "0",
    :inline-comment nil,
    :signature "#define MINO_VERSION_MAJOR 0",
    :doc
    "Compile-time version constants, bumped by the release process. Host code\ncan check these at preprocessor time to guard against embedding against an\nunexpected runtime:\n\n#if MINO_VERSION_MAJOR == 0 && MINO_VERSION_MINOR < 48\n# error \"embedded mino is too old; need >= 0.48\"\n#endif\n\nThe linked-in version (which may differ if the header was updated without\nrebuilding the runtime) is available at runtime via mino_version_string()."}
   {:kind :define,
    :name "MINO_VERSION_MINOR",
    :value "151",
    :inline-comment nil,
    :signature "#define MINO_VERSION_MINOR 151",
    :doc nil}
   {:kind :define,
    :name "MINO_VERSION_PATCH",
    :value "1",
    :inline-comment nil,
    :signature "#define MINO_VERSION_PATCH 1",
    :doc nil}
   {:kind :function,
    :name "mino_version_string",
    :return-type "const char *",
    :params "void",
    :signature "const char *mino_version_string(void);",
    :doc
    "Human-readable version string of the *linked* runtime, e.g. \"0.48.0\".\nThe pointer is to a static string with program lifetime; callers must not\nfree it."}]}
 {:name "Value types",
  :unstable false,
  :declarations
  [{:kind :enum,
    :name "mino_type_t",
    :variants
    [{:name "MINO_NIL", :comment nil}
     {:name "MINO_BOOL", :comment nil}
     {:name "MINO_INT", :comment nil}
     {:name "MINO_FLOAT", :comment "64-bit IEEE 754 double."}
     {:name "MINO_FLOAT32",
      :comment
      "32-bit IEEE 754 single. Distinct tag so float? matches both tiers and double? matches only MINO_FLOAT. Stored in the same `as.f` field as MINO_FLOAT (the 32-bit cast precision narrowing happens at construction); arithmetic always promotes to MINO_FLOAT, matching JVM Clojure where Float arithmetic yields Double."}
     {:name "MINO_CHAR", :comment "Unicode scalar value (codepoint)"}
     {:name "MINO_STRING", :comment nil}
     {:name "MINO_SYMBOL", :comment nil}
     {:name "MINO_KEYWORD", :comment nil}
     {:name "MINO_EMPTY_LIST",
      :comment
      "singleton: the canonical empty list `()`. Distinct from nil: seq?-true, nil?-false, empty?-true. Walkers terminate on it because mino_is_cons returns false. Construct via mino_empty_list(S)."}
     {:name "MINO_CONS", :comment nil}
     {:name "MINO_VECTOR", :comment nil}
     {:name "MINO_MAP", :comment nil}
     {:name "MINO_SET",
      :comment "persistent set: HAMT of keys with sentinel values"}
     {:name "MINO_SORTED_MAP",
      :comment "persistent sorted map: red-black tree"}
     {:name "MINO_SORTED_SET",
      :comment "persistent sorted set: red-black tree"}
     {:name "MINO_PRIM", :comment nil}
     {:name "MINO_FN", :comment nil}
     {:name "MINO_MACRO",
      :comment "user-defined macro (shares the fn struct layout)"}
     {:name "MINO_HANDLE",
      :comment "opaque host object: pointer + type tag string"}
     {:name "MINO_ATOM",
      :comment "mutable reference cell: wraps a single value"}
     {:name "MINO_VOLATILE",
      :comment
      "lightweight single-slot mutable cell. No watches, no validators, no atomic publish. Intended for transducer state where the reducing fn already implies single-thread access. Constructed via mino_volatile / `volatile!`."}
     {:name "MINO_LAZY",
      :comment
      "lazy sequence: thunk body + env, cached on first force"}
     {:name "MINO_CHUNK",
      :comment
      "fixed-capacity buffer of values used as the leaf of a chunked seq. Constructed via `chunk-buffer` (mutable, for accumulation) and sealed via `chunk` (returns the same value with sealed=1). After sealing, chunk-append throws."}
     {:name "MINO_CHUNKED_CONS",
      :comment
      "chunked-seq cell: holds one MINO_CHUNK plus an offset into it and a `more` seq for what follows the chunk. first/next/rest treat this transparently as a seq; chunk-first / chunk-rest expose the underlying chunk so combinators can pull a whole chunk at a time and emit chunked output. Constructed via `chunk-cons`."}
     {:name "MINO_RECUR",
      :comment "internal tail-call trampoline sentinel"}
     {:name "MINO_TAIL_CALL",
      :comment "proper tail call: carries {fn, args} for trampoline"}
     {:name "MINO_REDUCED",
      :comment "early termination wrapper for reduce"}
     {:name "MINO_VAR",
      :comment "first-class var: ns + name + root binding"}
     {:name "MINO_TRANSIENT",
      :comment
      "mutable staging wrapper for batch mutation of a persistent vector, map, or set. Embedders call mino_transient / mino_persistent and the *_bang accessors below."}
     {:name "MINO_BIGINT",
      :comment
      "arbitrary-precision signed integer. Backed by the vendored imath library (src/vendor/imath.c). The mpz_t struct is embedded in the value cell; the digit storage is owned by the cell and freed during GC sweep. See also `1N` literals and the `bigint` / `biginteger` constructors."}
     {:name "MINO_RATIO",
      :comment
      "arbitrary-precision rational number, stored as a numerator/denominator pair of bigints. The representation is always reduced (gcd = 1) and the denominator is always positive (sign lives on the numerator). See also `1/2` literals and the `numerator` / `denominator` primitives."}
     {:name "MINO_BIGDEC",
      :comment
      "arbitrary-precision decimal, stored as an unscaled bigint plus a non-negative integer scale: value = unscaled * 10^-scale. Constructed from `1.5M` / `1M` literals or via `(bigdec x)`."}
     {:name "MINO_TYPE",
      :comment
      "first-class record type. Carries identity (ptr), defining namespace, unqualified name, and an ordered vector of declared field-name keywords. Method tables are not stored here; protocol dispatch lives in the protocol's namespace, keyed by what (type x) returns. Construct via mino_defrecord."}
     {:name "MINO_RECORD",
      :comment
      "record value: pointer to its MINO_TYPE plus a malloc-owned array of field-value slots and an optional MINO_MAP for extension keys. Storage is field slots, not a backing map; map-iso behaviour (get, assoc, seq, count, ...) is a contract layered on top via primitive dispatch on this tag. Construct via mino_record."}
     {:name "MINO_FUTURE",
      :comment
      "host-thread future: carries a result-or-exception cell, a state machine (PENDING, RESOLVED, FAILED, CANCELLED), a mutex+cond for cross-thread delivery, and a thread handle. Promises share this type — a promise is a future that exposes `deliver` and never has a worker thread."}
     {:name "MINO_UUID",
      :comment
      "RFC 4122 UUID. 16 bytes inline. Equality is byte-wise; hash mixes the 16 bytes. The `#uuid \"...\"` reader literal and (random-uuid), (parse-uuid s) construct it."}
     {:name "MINO_REGEX",
      :comment
      "Compiled-on-use regex pattern. Holds the source string only -- mino's regex engine compiles per call. Equality is identity (matches Clojure JVM's `(= #\"x\" #\"x\")` returning false); print form is `#\"source\"`. The reader's `#\"...\"` literal and `(re-pattern s)` construct it."}
     {:name "MINO_MAP_ENTRY",
      :comment
      "Map entry: a (k, v) pair returned by first/seq of a map. Distinct from MINO_VECTOR -- key and val accept only MAP_ENTRY, throwing on plain 2-vectors -- but vector-shaped: vector?, coll?, sequential?, indexed?, counted?, associative?, reversible? all true; equality with a 2-vector compares element-wise. count is 2; nth 0 / 1 yield k / v; first/second/last/peek dispatch accordingly. Constructed by first/seq of a map and by `clojure.lang.MapEntry/create`."}
     {:name "MINO_HOST_ARRAY",
      :comment
      "JVM-style host array: a fixed-length container with element-kind tag (:object, :int, :long, etc.) for printing and zero-fill semantics. Distinct from MINO_VECTOR: predicates like vector?, coll?, sequential?, counted?, associative? all return false (matching JVM's Java arrays which don't implement IPersistentCollection). seq returns a chunked seq over the elements. Equality is identity. Constructed via object-array, int-array, long-array, etc."}
     {:name "MINO_TX_REF",
      :comment
      "Software transactional memory ref: identity cell holding a single committed value plus per-cell watches and validator. Mutations are confined to `dosync` transactions and serialized through a single-version optimistic protocol (read-set validation at commit; conflicts trigger retry). Equality is identity, matching atoms. Constructed via `(ref v)`. The MINO_REF symbol was already taken by the embedder rooting handle (mino_ref_t), so the enum tag is MINO_TX_REF; the Clojure-level type keyword is `:ref`."}
     {:name "MINO_AGENT",
      :comment
      "Asynchronous mutable cell with a per-state action run-queue. send / send-off enqueue (fn args) onto the queue and return the agent immediately; a worker thread drains the queue serially, applies actions under state_lock, and signals await waiters when the agent's in-flight count reaches zero. Watches and validators follow the atom/ref shape. Equality is identity. Constructed via `(agent v)`. The worker counts against thread_limit, so send / send-off throw MTH001 if the host hasn't granted a thread budget (default thread_limit == 1 means no agents)."}],
    :doc nil}
   {:kind :function,
    :name "mino_typeof",
    :return-type "mino_type_t",
    :params "const mino_val_t *v",
    :signature "mino_type_t mino_typeof(const mino_val_t *v);",
    :doc
    "Return the effective type of a value. Works for both heap-allocated\ncells and tagged scalars (int, bool, nil, char). NULL is treated as\nMINO_NIL. Use this to dispatch on type from C without reaching into\nthe value's internal layout."}
